<h2>The contractual stack</h2>
<p>The Indian company and its Korean counterpart built their relationship in three layers. A Technical Collaboration Licence Agreement of January 2019 covered wastewater treatment technology, with arbitration in Seoul under Korean law. A Joint Venture Agreement of October 2021 created an Indian joint venture company, with arbitration in Mumbai under Indian law. A Memorandum of Understanding of February 2023 engaged the Indian side for liaison and logistics services on a Mumbai municipal project, again with Mumbai arbitration.</p>
<p>When the Indian company alleged breach of the JVA&rsquo;s non-compete covenant — through the incorporation of a wholly owned Indian subsidiary — and claimed about Rs. 4.24 crore under the MoU along with Rs. 20 crore in business losses, it invoked the JVA and MoU clauses. The respondents answered that the disputes were really tied to the Licence Agreement, whose Korean clause should prevail; that a Korean arbitration was already afoot; that the subsidiary was a non-signatory; and that the agreed amicable settlement procedure had been skipped.</p>

<h2>What the Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Threshold only</h3><p>Section 11(6-A) restricts the referral inquiry to the prima facie existence of the arbitration agreement. Both sides accepted the JVA and MoU, and their clauses, exist — that settled the referral.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Different fields</h3><p>Prima facie, the three agreements operate in different fields. The JVA&rsquo;s and MoU&rsquo;s own clauses — fortified by their supersession provisions — govern disputes arising solely under them.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Overlap to the tribunal</h3><p>Whether particular claims actually belong to the Korean-seated licence arbitration is a question of the arbitrator&rsquo;s jurisdiction, not a ground to refuse appointment.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Non-signatory to the tribunal</h3><p>Whether the wholly owned subsidiary is bound was held contentious and left to the arbitrator — the referral court does not resolve group-of-companies questions at the gate.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The appointment</h2>
<p>Allowing both arbitration petitions, the Bench of Chief Justice Surya Kant and Justices Joymalya Bagchi and V. Mohana appointed a former Chief Justice of the Bombay High Court as sole arbitrator for the JVA and MoU disputes, with liberty to fix his own fees and no order as to costs.</p>

<h2>Why it matters for contract drafters and litigators</h2>
<p>For drafters, the case is a caution against dispute-resolution patchworks. Three agreements with three clauses, two seats and two governing laws guarantee a threshold fight before any merits are reached. Where a relationship is layered, the later documents should either adopt a single dispute-resolution architecture or state with precision which clause captures which disputes — the supersession clauses in the JVA and MoU did real work here.</p>
<p>For litigators resisting referral, the message is bleaker: objections built on overlap, non-signatories or skipped pre-arbitral steps will rarely stop a Section 11 petition. The realistic strategy is to preserve those objections for the tribunal under Section 16, where they can be developed on evidence rather than compressed into a threshold hearing.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The judgment continues the Supreme Court&rsquo;s steady narrowing of the referral stage: show a prima facie arbitration agreement, and the parties go to arbitration; everything else — scope, overlap, parties, procedure — is argued before the arbitrator, not instead of one.</p>
</div>
